	<p>This notice covers the standard functions of the systopic framework and fluxo: signing in, managing your account and editing content in the administration interface. It does not cover visitors to the public website or additional project-specific integrations.</p>
	<p>The controller is the responsible operator identified above or in the linked <a class="cmsExternalLink" href="<?= htmlspecialchars($imprint, ENT_QUOTES) ?>" target="_blank" rel="noopener">imprint<i class="cmsIcon cmsIcon-arrow-up-right-from-square" aria-hidden="true"></i></a>. The operator's contact details, any appointed data protection officer, hosting arrangements and supplementary privacy information are available in the <a class="cmsExternalLink" href="<?= htmlspecialchars($privacy, ENT_QUOTES) ?>" target="_blank" rel="noopener">operator's privacy policy<i class="cmsIcon cmsIcon-arrow-up-right-from-square" aria-hidden="true"></i></a>. systopic GmbH supplies the software; that alone does not make it the controller or a recipient of this installation's account data.</p>
	<p>This notice describes the software defaults; the linked operator information must identify the actual controller, applicable legal bases, providers and retention rules of this installation. It is information about processing, not a request for consent. Signing in does not waive your data protection rights.</p>
    <h2>Independent installation and email delivery</h2>
    <p>This software is installed and operated as a separate installation. Supplying the system packages does not by itself create a centrally hosted systopic account service or a central mail service. The operator configures its own hosting, database and SMTP connection in the installation's environment configuration, including any machine-local overrides. SMTP credentials are server-side configuration; they are not part of this public notice and must not be published with project source code.</p>
    <p>Messages are sent through that configured SMTP server to their recipients. The mail provider can therefore process recipient/sender addresses, message contents and delivery metadata as needed for delivery. The actual provider and processing arrangements belong in the linked operator information; they cannot be inferred merely from the use of SMTP. An installation outside DEV refuses to send through the standard mail class if no SMTP host is configured. DEV without a configured SMTP server uses a local mail catcher.</p>
    <p><b>Mail test routing:</b> DEV always redirects the standard mail class's To, CC and BCC envelope to the configured test recipient. The Mail block in Environment settings can also enable this mode for STAGING or LIVE and set the recipient. An empty recipient uses the system default testmail@fluxo.systems. This also applies to the local DEV mail catcher. Reply addresses are replaced and the subject is marked as test mail. A malformed test recipient blocks delivery. Test mailboxes receive message contents and attachments; operators must choose an authorised mailbox and account for its provider when processing personal or confidential information. Test routing does not anonymise message contents. Normal delivery resumes outside DEV only when explicit test mode is off.</p>
	<h2>Accounts and authentication</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>The system processes your account name, email address, profile information and permissions. Changes to content can be associated with the account that created or last edited it.</li>
		<li>Passwords are checked during sign-in; the account stores a password hash rather than the password as entered.</li>
		<li>If you add a passkey, the account stores its credential identifier, public key, chosen name, usage counter and timestamps. The private key and any fingerprint or face data used to unlock it remain with your device or passkey provider and are not sent to the System.</li>
		<li>Invitation, recovery and remote-login codes are checked using stored hashes. Remote login also processes the account and device being connected and the account that approves it. QR scanning uses the camera locally in the browser; the standard scanner does not upload or store camera frames.</li>
		<li>Invitation, password recovery and account-security emails use your email address and relevant account information through the mail service configured by the operator. Passwords and private passkey keys are not included.</li>
	</ul>
	<h2>Cookies and browser storage</h2>
	<p>The following storage supports authentication, the current editing session and interface settings. It is not used by the standard System for advertising or cross-site audience measurement.</p>
	<ul>
		<li><b>Session cookie:</b> links requests to the current session. Its name and lifetime depend on this installation's PHP/session configuration; it is normally a browser-session cookie. Server-side session expiry can end access earlier.</li>
		<li><b>Device cookie:</b> contains a device identifier and random secret to recognise a signed-in device. The browser cookie is renewed with a maximum lifetime of <?= (int) Devices::COOKIE_DAYS ?> days. This does not guarantee access for that time: person devices expire after <?= (int) $deviceDays ?> days without use in this installation. <?php if ($screenDays === null) { ?>Screen accounts remain authorised until revoked or otherwise disabled.<?php } else { ?>Screen devices expire after <?= (int) $screenDays ?> days without use.<?php } ?> Signing out or revoking a device invalidates its access. Device records contain a device name, browser/user-agent details, client mode, timestamps and the last IP address.</li>
		<li><b>Remote-login cookie (<code>sys_pair</code>, with a project suffix for installations in subdirectories):</b> holds a random polling secret for the temporary pairing process. It expires with that process, normally after ten minutes. <code>sys_plain</code> is a temporary display flag, valid for up to ten minutes or removed on the next normal System visit.</li>
		<li><b>Display cookies:</b> <code>cmsTimezone</code> stores the browser's time zone for date display, and <code>cmsMobile</code> stores the compact-layout flag, each for up to one year and renewed when set again. Where supplied by the client, <code>sys_display</code> reports whether the interface runs in a browser or installed-app mode.</li>
		<li><b>Local Storage:</b> <code>cmsDevice</code>, <code>cmsDeviceMobile</code> and <code>cmsSidebarClosed</code> remember your selected preview widths and collapsed property sections. They have no automatic expiry and remain until overwritten or the site's browser data are cleared.</li>
		<li><b>Session Storage:</b> <code>cmsDoc</code> and <code>cmsOpenSheet</code> preserve mobile window navigation within the browser tab. The pending navigation value is consumed when used; session storage is normally removed when the tab's session ends.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>You can clear these entries in your browser's site-data settings. Clearing authentication cookies can sign you out, and blocking them can prevent sign-in. The operator must distinguish storage strictly necessary for a service you request from optional convenience features; mentioning a feature here does not replace any consent required under § 25 TDDDG.</p>
	<h2>Login log and security</h2>
	<p>Authentication events, including sign-ins, failed attempts, sign-outs, invitations and remote approvals, are logged with the time, account name or email address, IP address, action and sign-in method where available. An event can also identify the approving account or invitation recipient. Authorised administrators can access the log to investigate misuse and account-access problems. The standard interface restricts manual log clearing to superusers; requests for deletion must therefore be handled by the operator through an appropriately authorised person. Failed attempts can be counted by IP address to limit guessing.</p>
	<p>The fixed system retention rule makes login-log entries older than <?= (int) \Systopic\System\Maintenance\LogRetention::LOGIN_DAYS ?> days eligible for automatic deletion by maintenance. Normal site requests trigger bounded cleanup steps; an optional CLI or Webcron scheduler can run them independently of visits. Without requests or a functioning scheduler, physical deletion can occur later. A full backlog can require several runs. Large, unreadable or malformed log files require administrator review; cleanup failures and remaining work must be checked in Maintenance. The operator must monitor successful cleanup rather than treating this threshold as a guaranteed deletion time. This period is built into the system and is not a per-project setting. It is not a statutory minimum or a blanket authorisation to retain every event for that long; superusers can delete entries earlier when their purpose ends. Retention depends on the operator's documented need to detect and investigate unauthorised access, resolve authentication incidents and establish relevant account activity. The assessment must consider the detection and investigation window, whether an incident remains unresolved, the sensitivity and volume of the information and whether less identifying information would serve the same purpose. Storage capacity, a possible future use or the absence of a fixed statutory deadline alone do not justify continued retention.</p>
	<p>The operator must define and document review intervals and, where determinable, deletion periods for this installation and make the applicable rules available in the linked operator information. When the relevant purpose ends, entries must be deleted or effectively anonymised without undue delay unless a specific legal obligation or another lawful, necessary purpose requires retention. Only records relevant to a concrete incident, statutory obligation or the establishment, exercise or defence of legal claims may be retained for that additional purpose, with restricted access and a documented end or review criterion. This does not justify keeping the entire log for the longest conceivable claim period.</p>
	<p>Temporary session-name markers help attribute an expired session. The existing cleanup removes markers older than <?= (int) LoginLog::KEEP_DAYS ?> days when a new session is created and through the maintenance tasks. By default, normal requests trigger bounded maintenance batches; without requests there is no automatic execution. An operator may additionally configure CLI or Webcron scheduling. The request fallback is not a scheduled daily deletion. Physical removal can occur later than the eligibility threshold, depending on requests, remaining batches and the configured scheduler. This technical cleanup threshold does not determine retention of the login log itself.</p>
	<h2>Live updates and collaboration</h2>
	<p>Where enabled, the installation uses a WebSocket connection to its configured Centrifugo hub for live updates, device-access changes and online or editing status. The connection processes network information such as your IP address, an authenticated project/account identifier, temporary access tokens and subscriptions identifying the data or editing context you may access. Change signals cause authorised browsers to request the updated content from the installation. Account and activity information shown to other authorised users depends on the enabled collaboration functions and their permissions.</p>
	<p>The hub is infrastructure configured by the operator; using the Centrifugo software does not by itself send account data to its developer. If the operator uses a separate hosting or managed hub provider, that provider, processing locations, access arrangements and any applicable international-transfer safeguards must be identified in the operator information. Sharing a hub between projects does not make their account information generally accessible to each other. Connection, proxy and server logs may exist independently of the live messages; the operator must specify their actual retention and restrict access.</p>
	<h2>Optional remote diagnostics</h2>
	<p>When debugging and live connections are enabled, remote diagnostics can register a browser before sign-in, including requests outside the administration interface where the diagnostic bootstrap runs. It sets the HTTP-only cookie <code>sysdebug_client</code>, containing a random browser identifier with a browser lifetime of up to one year. The diagnostic registry associates that identifier with browser/user-agent details, IP address, most recent request URL, account identifier where signed in, and last-seen time. Request URLs may include query parameters; personal or confidential information must not be placed in URLs unnecessarily.</p>
	<p>The diagnostic interface lists browsers seen within the last 30 minutes. Registry entries inactive for more than 24 hours and expired diagnostic sessions are removed when the registry is updated or its maintenance task runs. Normal requests can trigger bounded maintenance; an operator may additionally configure CLI or Webcron scheduling. These are cleanup thresholds, not guaranteed physical deletion deadlines. A viewing session expires after approximately two minutes without renewal. While a superuser has an authorised diagnostic window open, debug messages from the selected browser's requests can be streamed through the configured hub. Their content depends on enabled debug output and can include request or application data. Closing or expiry ends the diagnostic session; it does not erase independently retained server logs, copies or exports.</p>
	<p>Remote diagnostics are for specific troubleshooting and secure operation, not routine employee performance monitoring. The operator must assess necessity and the applicable legal basis, limit diagnostic output and access, avoid recording credentials or unnecessary content, and disable diagnostics when no longer needed. Any consent required for non-essential browser storage must be obtained before that storage is used; this notice does not implement a consent mechanism. A production installation must not assume that enabling debug mode makes all resulting collection lawful.</p>
    <h2>Local software and installation-specific providers</h2>
    <p>Browser libraries and fonts supplied with this installation are served locally; their use alone does not make their developers recipients of your account or uploaded-file data. The Tabler Outline, Phosphor Duotone and the monochrome Solar info icon are also served by this installation; displaying them does not connect your browser to Tabler, Phosphor, Solar or Iconify. Their licence notices and icon attribution are provided under License. External network services remain a separate matter as described below.</p>
    <p>The operator information linked at the top must name the actual hosting, mail and any externally operated live-update providers, the relevant processing locations, legal bases, and any required international-transfer safeguards. These vary by project and cannot be inferred from the software vendor or the fact that a provider's software is installed. Missing provider details must be completed by the operator before deployment; the links do not make an incomplete project policy complete.</p>
	<h2>External services inside the System</h2>
	<p>A direct browser request to another provider necessarily exposes your IP address and technical request information, such as browser details and, depending on the browser's referrer policy, the referring origin or page. The System does not deliberately add your account password or private passkey key to these requests. Providers can nevertheless associate requests with other information, for example an existing provider login.</p>
	<ul>
		<li><b>Profile pictures:</b> uploaded photos are served by this installation. Accounts without an uploaded photo receive a locally generated DiceBear avatar, stored in this installation’s avatar folder. For people, a SHA-256 digest of the trimmed, lower-case email address selects a repeatable Notionists image; without an email, an account identifier is used. Screens use Bottts with an account-derived seed. The same email, generator version, style and options produce the same person avatar across installations. The digest is pseudonymous, not anonymous, and is not an authentication secret. An email or theme change can regenerate the cached image. No email address, seed or image request is sent to DiceBear or another avatar service. If local generation or storage fails, a local user or screen icon is shown. Uploaded photos take precedence.</li>
		<li><b>Language flags:</b> the standard System serves its bundled and project-uploaded SVG flags locally. Displaying a language flag does not contact Flagcdn, Flagpedia or another external flag service. The download link in Language settings opens the external SVG source only when selected.</li>
		<li><b>YouTube and Vimeo in the media editor:</b> opening an external video in the standard file editor first shows a local placeholder. The player is loaded only after you click its Load player button. This choice applies to that view; reopening the editor requires another click. No player request is made by that placeholder before the click. YouTube uses <code>youtube-nocookie.com</code>; Vimeo uses <code>player.vimeo.com</code> with <code>dnt=1</code>. Once loaded, these options do not prevent network requests or guarantee that no personal data or device information are processed. The provider receives the video identifier and request information and can process playback interactions. See <a class="cmsExternalLink" href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google/YouTube's privacy information<i class="cmsIcon cmsIcon-arrow-up-right-from-square" aria-hidden="true"></i></a> and <a class="cmsExternalLink" href="https://vimeo.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Vimeo's privacy information<i class="cmsIcon cmsIcon-arrow-up-right-from-square" aria-hidden="true"></i></a>. This description concerns the System editor, not the public website's player or consent settings.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>These services and their delivery networks may process data outside the EU/EEA. The operator must determine the applicable legal basis and any required consent before enabling them, and identify the actual recipients and applicable transfer safeguards for this installation. The supplementary information must state whether an adequacy decision applies or which appropriate safeguards support the transfer and how a copy can be obtained. A provider link or a privacy-enhanced player setting does not itself establish those safeguards or collect consent. Where prior consent is required, requests must be prevented until it has been given; this notice does not implement such a block. Additional requests made by project templates shown in the System preview require supplementary information from the operator.</p>
	<h2>Purposes, legal bases and recipients</h2>
	<p>Account and authentication processing provides authorised access and the editing functions requested by you or your organisation. Art. 6 (1) (b) GDPR applies where processing is necessary to perform a contract with you; Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR may apply to secure operation and preventing misuse, subject to the operator's assessment. A contract with your employer or organisation does not by itself make Art. 6 (1) (b) GDPR applicable to your personal data. Employment-related use can require a different or supplementary legal basis determined by the operator. Any statutory obligation relied on under Art. 6 (1) (c) GDPR must be identified by the operator; it is not presumed for all account or log data. Where processing requires consent, the applicable basis is Art. 6 (1) (a) GDPR, and you may withdraw that consent with future effect. The legal basis for external services must be assessed separately from the basis for account security.</p>
	<p>Recipients can include authorised account administrators, the operator's hosting and mail providers, and the external services described above when requested by your browser. Access by service providers must be limited to their agreed tasks. The operator identifies its actual providers, processing locations and any international-transfer arrangements in its supplementary information.</p>
	<h2>Storage and your rights</h2>
	<p>Account and permission data are kept while needed to provide and administer your access; the operator reviews and deletes accounts that are no longer needed. Passkeys and device records are managed with that account, but an expired or revoked device is not necessarily deleted immediately. Content and its editing attribution follow the operator's content-retention rules. The login-log retention criteria and browser-storage lifetimes are stated above. Technical email delivery logs (including stored message contents), PHP error logs and backup execution logs become eligible for automatic cleanup after 90 days under the same bounded maintenance mechanism described above. Messages marked sensitive by the sending code omit their contents from the email log. Superusers can inspect messages addressed to an account’s current email address in its E-Mail Log tab. Technical logs do not replace a legally required business archive: correspondence, invoices and other records requiring longer retention must be stored separately for their applicable purpose and period. Undated legacy entries and unreadable or oversized logs require administrator review. The operator separately specifies retention for provider-side logs, mailboxes and backup archives. Backup copies subject to a justified recovery cycle must be protected against ordinary use, expire under a defined schedule and have applicable deletion or restriction decisions reapplied after restoration. A backup is not an exception permitting unlimited retention. There is no general permission to keep personal data indefinitely merely because storage is available.</p>
	<p>Subject to the applicable conditions, you can ask the operator for access, correction, deletion, restriction and data portability. Where processing relies on Art. 6 (1) (f) GDPR, you may object on grounds relating to your particular situation. The operator must then stop that processing unless it demonstrates compelling legitimate grounds overriding your interests, rights and freedoms, or needs the data for legal claims. Where processing relies on consent, you may withdraw it at any time without affecting the lawfulness of earlier processing. Portability applies where the statutory conditions are met, including automated processing based on consent or a contract. You can complain to a competent data protection supervisory authority, in particular where you live or work or where an alleged infringement occurred. The operator's contact details are linked above. An account and a supported way of signing in are necessary for System access; without the necessary account and authentication data, that access cannot be provided. Optional profile information and optional sign-in methods are not a general prerequisite. Data can be provided by you, by an administrator who creates or manages your account, or automatically by your browser and authentication device as described above. The standard authentication functions do not make decisions under Art. 22 GDPR producing legal or similarly significant effects.</p>
	</div>
